\cf2 \
You have a sixth nerve palsy in one eye. This means the sixth cranial nerve, which controls the lateral rectus muscle, is not working normally.\
\
The lateral rectus muscle moves the eye outward. When it is weak, the eye may turn inward and double vision is often worse when looking toward the affected side or when looking at distance. Many people turn their head toward the affected side to reduce the double vision.\
\
A sixth nerve palsy can occur for several reasons, including reduced blood flow to the nerve, increased pressure inside the skull, trauma, or other neurological conditions. The cause determines how urgently further testing is needed.\
\
